cff-version: 1.2.0
message: 'If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata.'
title: 'IELTS API: a free, no-authentication REST API and open dataset for IELTS preparation research'
type: software
version: 1.4.0
date-released: '2026-09-05'
abstract: >-
  IELTS API is an open, dependency-free TypeScript REST API that exposes
  machine-readable IELTS preparation data: 4,174 headwords extracted from the
  Cambridge IELTS volumes 1-22 vocabulary lists, condensed analytic band
  descriptors for Speaking and Writing across bands 0-9, indicative score
  concordances for CEFR, TOEFL iBT, the Cambridge English Scale, PTE Academic
  and the Duolingo English Test, Writing and Speaking task banks, a canonical
  taxonomy of the thirteen IELTS Reading and Listening question types with the
  frequencies observed in 27,225 practice questions, a structure and
  readability index of 1,702 practice tests and CEFR-graded reading lessons,
  fifty recurring exam themes, a curated index of an open IELTS research
  corpus, and a grey-literature archive index: the Cambridge IELTS 1-18
  listening audio catalogued by naming era, media archaeology and completeness,
  the twelve official sample tasks measured for readability, and 24 marked
  learner essays summarised as derived statistics, and a mock-exam test-centre
  index of an operational IELTS online test centre: 377 self-marking papers
  around an unbroken Cambridge IELTS 4-21 across listening, reading and
  writing, a Cambridge holdings matrix, 1,099 hand-tagged question groups
  carrying
  canonical question type, teaching scene and difficulty for 5,408 questions,
  and the centre's production raw-score-to-band calibration. Deterministic analysis tools profile any text for Flesch readability,
  lexical diversity and Cambridge-headword coverage, and compose the datasets
  into a week-by-week study plan towards a target band. Every endpoint is free
  of charge,
  requires no authentication, returns a uniform JSON envelope with a stable
  ETag, and is covered by a test suite with 100% statement, branch, function
  and line coverage. The API is designed for reproducible second-language
  assessment research: responses are deterministic, versioned and citable.
keywords:
